const details = [
  ["The frontier contains generated states waiting for examination; a strategy is the rule that removes one waiting state next.","The same state space produces different traces when DFS, BFS, or UCS organizes that frontier differently.","Uninformed means no heuristic h(n); it does not remove the successor function, goal test, or frontier discipline.","Lesson 4 adds frontier policy, duplicate handling, goal timing, and path records to the state-space model."],
  ["DFS chooses depth, BFS chooses a level, bounded search limits depth, UCS chooses accumulated cost, and backtracking exposes path recovery.","Completeness asks whether a solution will be found; optimality asks whether it is best; time and space measure different resources.","Finite branching, step costs, depth bounds, duplicate policy, and memory turn those guarantees on or off.","Match shallow equal-cost goals to BFS, tight memory to DFS, unknown depth to deepening, and variable costs to UCS."],
  ["A real program starts with A and generates later nodes only by applying legal operators to removed states.","DFS and BFS see the same children but place and remove them in different orders.","Generated means waiting on OPEN; examined means removed, tested, and processed. Those events are not interchangeable.","Locate S in both removal orders and mark when it is removed under the stated goal-test policy."],
  ["Before comparing pictures, read the selected state, OPEN, CLOSED, and the event named by each label.","The BFS picture is after expanding C, not another iteration-six snapshot.","Visual similarity cannot normalize two different algorithmic moments; the event context is part of the evidence.","A fair comparison aligns expansion count, selected node, frontier order, explored set, and goal timing."],
  ["Deepest means greatest depth among waiting states, so descendants outrank older siblings.","Prepending children produces LIFO stack behavior and a narrow stored frontier.","That memory advantage permits infinite descent, cycles, and a long first solution while a shallow goal waits elsewhere.","Use DFS when depth is plausible and memory is tight, not when shortest-path guarantees are required."],
  ["OPEN begins with Start and CLOSED begins empty; the loop continues until OPEN is exhausted.","Execute the enlarged lines in order: remove X, test X, generate children, close X, filter duplicates, and prepend survivors.","Checking both OPEN and CLOSED prevents pending duplicates and repeated examination.","Failure occurs only when OPEN is empty; returning a path additionally requires parent pointers."],
  ["The leftmost OPEN entry is always next; that invariant comes from prepending children.","When B expands, E and F move ahead of older alternatives C and D.","OPEN and CLOSED describe search status, but parent pointers explain how the goal was reached.","Verify one row transition by accounting for the removal, new children, CLOSED update, and parents."],
  ["At iteration one, A is closed and B is the leftmost waiting state.","After B expands, E and F are prepended before C and D, exposing stack behavior.","Choosing C would reveal queue reasoning; choosing alphabetically would invent a rule the algorithm never stated.","Narrate the next update with remove, test, expand, and prepend."],
  ["Shallowest means minimum depth from the initial state; every depth-n state precedes depth n plus one.","A FIFO queue keeps older frontier states ahead of newly generated children.","Finite branching gives completeness, and equal step costs make a shallowest goal minimum cost.","The exponential breadth frontier can still exhaust memory before the guarantee becomes useful."],
  ["Initialization, removal, testing, expansion, CLOSED, duplicate filtering, and failure match DFS.","Only the insertion end changes: surviving children join the right of OPEN.","That queue invariant—not the word queue by itself—explains level-order removal.","After B expands, C and D remain ahead of E and F because they arrived earlier."],
  ["B, C, and D enter at depth one, so all remain ahead of E and F at depth two.","C stays next after B expands because of arrival order, not alphabetical order.","Testing a goal on generation versus removal changes the stopping event and can change an optimality argument.","Find the first row where depth one is closed and identify the depth-two frontier order."],
  ["After A expands, B is the oldest waiting state and therefore the next removal.","Expanding B appends E and F, producing C, D, E, F rather than letting new children cut the line.","The next step moves C to CLOSED and appends G and H.","Compare this queue with the DFS trace and identify their first forced disagreement."],
  ["The puzzle numbers show removal order; BFS reaches a shallow goal only after earlier frontier entries.","Follow parent pointers backward from the goal, then reverse the chain to recover forward actions.","Reversible moves create duplicate boards, so graph search suppresses copies already on OPEN or CLOSED.","Point to a repeated board and explain why shortest parent information must be retained."],
  ["BFS orders by depth; DFS orders by active branch, changing both memory and first-solution behavior.","BFS may store a whole breadth layer, while DFS usually stores a path and alternatives.","BFS fits shallow equal-cost goals; DFS fits deep goals under tight memory but gives up optimality.","Change goal depth, branching, memory, or cost and explain why the recommendation changes."],
  ["Case one favors BFS because shallowest depth equals minimum cost when every step costs the same.","Case two favors DFS's narrow memory footprint while accepting a possibly nonshortest result.","Case three favors iterative deepening because goal depth is unknown.","Case four requires UCS because total path cost, not depth, defines the best solution."],
  ["A cutoff says the chosen bound blocked deeper expansion; it does not prove that no deeper solution exists.","Iterative deepening runs complete bounded searches with limits zero, one, two, and onward.","Most nodes in a broad tree lie near the deepest level, so repeating small upper levels can be affordable.","Space is roughly b times d while time remains exponential in solution depth."],
  ["The depth-five boundary hides every descendant below it, including a possible goal at depth six.","Green DFS follows a branch; red iterative deepening revisits upper layers before extending the limit.","Repeated shallow work can cost less than one disastrous descent when branching is broad and the goal is shallow.","Use branching factor and shallowest goal depth to justify that tradeoff."],
  ["UCS stores g(n), the accumulated path cost, and removes the frontier state with the smallest value.","Insert priced children, remove the cheapest entry, and update a state when a cheaper parent path is discovered.","A generated goal can be expensive; positive costs make it optimal when removed as the cheapest frontier entry.","If every edge costs one, g(n) equals depth and UCS reproduces BFS layers."],
  ["The table contains conditional claims, not unconditional badges.","BFS needs finite branching and equal costs for its strongest guarantees; DFS promises neither in infinite depth.","A depth limit can hide a goal; deepening repairs that; UCS requires costs bounded by a positive epsilon.","Read b as branching, d as shallowest goal depth, m as maximum depth, and l as the limit."],
  ["Backtracking keeps a current path until a goal, dead end, or unacceptable cost forces recovery.","Recovery selects the nearest ancestor with an untried alternative.","DFS also returns to alternatives, but this version exposes path, alternatives, dead ends, and optional cost rejection.","Dead-end memory helps pruning but is not a heuristic estimate of remaining goal distance."],
  ["SL is the current path and CS is its current state.","NSL records available new states; DE records known dead ends.","When CS has no viable child, shrink SL until an ancestor has an available alternative.","Trace one failure by updating DE, shortening SL, selecting from NSL, and assigning CS."],
  ["Ancestor records encode logical recovery without requiring a physical reverse action in the represented world.","A failed state enters DE, SL contracts, and NSL supplies the next alternative.","Search bookkeeping and external-world motion are therefore separate mechanisms.","Compare adjacent trace rows in the order CS, SL, NSL, and DE."],
  ["At iteration zero, CS and SL begin at A while DE is empty.","By iteration three, the path has grown and alternatives remain recorded.","At iteration five, failed states appear in DE while SL shows the surviving path.","At iteration eight, identify the failed state, contracted ancestor, selected alternative, and DE update."],
  ["Graph DFS centers OPEN and CLOSED; backtracking centers SL, NSL, DE, and CS.","DFS recovery selects a waiting OPEN state; backtracking explicitly shrinks the path.","Their pruning signals are duplicate history versus dead ends or unacceptable cost.","Both remain depth-oriented and can still require exponential exploration."],
  ["Begin with a real problem whose goals, constraints, observations, and legal actions are explicit.","The state space is conceptual; the graph turns states into nodes and legal transitions into arcs.","Data structures store the graph, frontier, history, costs, and parents; the algorithm updates them.","The solution may be a goal, a path, or both, so each stage must preserve needed information."],
  ["The representation tab connects states, state spaces, graphs, operators, and data structures through one modeled problem.","The direction tab distinguishes initial state, goal, path, forward search, and backward search.","The algorithms tab requires DFS, BFS, and backtracking mechanisms, invariants, benefits, and limitations.","Answer one weak item conceptually and then with a trace or implementation detail."],
  ["Outcomes one through three cover representation from state space to computational model.","Outcomes four through six require implementing and tracing DFS, BFS, and backtracking.","DFS and BFS are brute force because neither uses an h(n) estimate of remaining goal distance.","Dead-end information helps backtracking, but worst-case growth can remain exponential."],
  ["The trace must show selection, children, ordered OPEN, CLOSED, goal timing, and parent information.","The comparison must pair each selection rule with a conditional guarantee.","Diagnose one cycle, depth, memory, hidden-goal, or cost failure and choose the matching repair.","Problem conditions come before the algorithm name; heuristics in Lesson 5 add estimates, not permission to skip modeling."],
];
const workedExamples = [
  "Suppose OPEN contains B at depth two, C at depth one, and D at depth three. DFS selects D, BFS selects C, and UCS ignores depth unless the path costs make one entry cheapest. The state labels did not change; only the priority rule did.",
  "Consider a delivery robot. A shallow equal-cost hallway map supports BFS, a massive deep warehouse under a memory cap may justify DFS, variable travel times require UCS, and an unknown shelf depth makes iterative deepening attractive. One application can demand different strategies after one condition changes.",
  "Starting from A, the program applies the successor function to create B, C, and D. It does not allocate every descendant in the picture. When B is removed, only then are E and F generated. Incremental generation is why frontier management matters operationally.",
  "Imagine comparing a photo taken after six DFS removals with a photo taken immediately after BFS expands C. Different OPEN contents are expected because both the algorithm and timestamp differ. Relabeling the pictures with the exact event prevents a false conclusion about which method expanded more nodes.",
  "With OPEN equal to B, C, D, DFS removes B. If B generates E and F, prepending produces E, F, C, D. The next removal is E, then E's child if one exists. C waits even though it is shallower and was generated earlier.",
  "Let OPEN be B, C, D. Removing B and generating E and F gives E, F, C, D after duplicate filtering. B moves to CLOSED. If E was already waiting on OPEN, it must not be inserted again. Parent records still remember which surviving child came from B.",
  "Read the transition from OPEN B, C, D and CLOSED A. After B expands, OPEN becomes E, F, C, D and CLOSED becomes B, A. The front changed because children were prepended; the history changed because B was examined; the path map changes because E and F point to B.",
  "Before clicking Next, cover the screen and predict all fields. At iteration two the selected state should be B, OPEN should begin with E and F, and CLOSED should begin with B. Revealing the state tests the entire update rule, not merely the next node label.",
  "For the same OPEN B, C, D, BFS removes B and appends E and F, producing C, D, E, F. C stays first because it entered before E and F. That simple queue invariant generates complete level order across the whole tree.",
  "Compare two nearly identical lines: DFS places remaining children on the left; BFS places them on the right. With children E and F and older alternatives C and D, left insertion yields E, F, C, D, while right insertion yields C, D, E, F.",
  "After A expands, the depth-one states B, C, D wait in that order. Expanding B appends depth-two states E and F. Even if E looks promising, BFS removes C next because C is older and shallower. The trace visualizes that invariant row by row.",
  "Clicking three times should move CLOSED from A to B, A and then C, B, A, while new children accumulate at OPEN's right edge. If a new child appears at the left, the implementation has silently changed from queue behavior to stack behavior.",
  "In an eight-puzzle, moving a blank right and later left can recreate an earlier board. CLOSED detects the examined duplicate; OPEN detects a pending duplicate. Once the numbered goal is removed, following parent links backward yields the exact shortest move sequence under equal move costs.",
  "Take branching factor ten and a goal at depth six. BFS may retain hundreds of thousands of frontier nodes near the deepest layer. DFS may store only a path and alternatives, but could chase an unrelated branch far beyond depth six. The resource trade is concrete, not rhetorical.",
  "Case three demonstrates why a guessed bound is fragile. A limit of four misses a goal at depth five; a huge limit recreates DFS's descent risk. Iterative deepening systematically tries four, then five, paying repeated upper-level work to avoid both guesses.",
  "If the bound is two, a node at depth two is examined but its children are cut off. The result is cutoff, not ordinary failure. Iterative deepening reruns with bound three, allowing those children to enter the frontier without storing every earlier breadth layer simultaneously.",
  "Suppose each state has four children and the shallowest goal is depth five. Most generated states live at depth five, so re-expanding depths zero through four adds relatively little compared with storing the entire breadth frontier. A narrow branching factor changes that calculation.",
  "From A, imagine edges to B cost two, C cost five, and D cost one. UCS removes D first even if D is visually deeper. If a later route reaches C for cost three, the priority record for C must be decreased from five to three.",
  "A depth limit l of five uses space proportional to b times l, but fails if the only goal is at six. UCS is optimal with positive costs, yet a tiny epsilon can still allow many low-cost expansions. The symbols describe growth patterns, not guaranteed wall-clock comfort.",
  "In a maze, the current path may be A, B, F. If F is a dead end, record F, return logically to B, and try B's next unvisited alternative. If B has none, continue shrinking toward A. The saved alternatives decide where recovery stops.",
  "Suppose CS is F, SL is F, B, A, NSL contains F, B, C, D, A, and DE contains E, I, H. If F has no viable child, add F to DE, shrink SL, and choose the next alternative associated with the nearest surviving ancestor.",
  "A delivery robot need not physically reverse every meter merely because the planning algorithm backtracks. The planner can retract a hypothetical route in memory, select another branch, and then send only the chosen forward route to the robot for execution.",
  "Scrub from iteration three to five and watch two coupled changes: SL loses the failed suffix while DE gains those failed states. NSL preserves alternatives. The current state CS should always agree with the active end of the recovered path.",
  "Both methods may visit A, B, F and then recover. Graph DFS already has C and D waiting in OPEN. The list-based backtracker instead shrinks SL and consults NSL. Similar traversal order does not imply identical records or recovery code.",
  "For route planning, the real problem specifies destinations and road rules. A state encodes location and relevant conditions. The graph turns legal moves into arcs. A priority queue and hash set implement the frontier and history. Parent pointers turn the goal into directions.",
  "Answer the queue question operationally: after B expands, which list changes, where do its children enter, and which state leaves next? A correct answer ties the data structure to the visible trace. Saying only queue or stack is a label without a mechanism.",
  "A student who can define BFS but cannot predict OPEN after one expansion has not yet met the trace outcome. A student who can trace but cannot store parents has not yet met the implementation outcome. The grid separates those competencies so practice can target the gap.",
  "If search loops between two states, add duplicate detection. If DFS descends forever, add a bound or deepening. If BFS exhausts memory, reconsider representation or strategy. If a shallow path is expensive, use cost ordering. Each repair addresses a different diagnosed cause.",
];
const deepDives = [
  "Think of uninformed search as entering a museum after the lights fail. You still have the room map, the door rules, and a test for recognizing the exit; what you lack is an arrow saying warmer or colder. DFS, BFS, and UCS are therefore not guesses. They are disciplined ways to organize the same waiting list. The comedy begins only when someone calls a queue random because it refuses to read minds.",
  "Treat the four evaluation questions like a job interview for algorithms. Completeness asks whether the candidate eventually finishes the assignment. Optimality asks whether the result is the best acceptable one. Time asks how much work accumulates, and space asks how much must remain on the desk at once. A charming acronym does not get hired; the problem conditions and guarantees do. Even algorithms need references, although theirs are usually asymptotic.",
  "Incremental generation resembles building a city only as travelers reach each intersection. The planner does not pour concrete for every possible street before leaving home. It creates the start, applies legal operators, and materializes successors when their parent is examined. That saves work, but it also makes order consequential: a state that waits deep in OPEN has not yet donated its children to the graph. The visible tree is a teaching summary, not a preloaded warehouse inventory.",
  "A trace without an event label is like a laboratory photo without a timestamp. It may be perfectly sharp and still support the wrong conclusion. To compare DFS and BFS fairly, freeze both after the same kind of event: state removed, goal tested, children inserted, or expansion count completed. Then compare selected state, ordered OPEN, and CLOSED. Otherwise we are timing one runner at kilometer six and another after tying a shoe, then publishing a very confident sports report.",
  "DFS behaves like a spelunker who chooses one tunnel, keeps a compact rope back to the entrance, and postpones neighboring tunnels. This is why memory can remain modest: the active path and alternatives matter more than a whole breadth layer. The risk is equally physical. A tunnel may be extremely long, cyclic, or bottomless in the model. Courage is not completeness; without loop checks or a bound, the brave spelunker can become a permanent resident of the left branch.",
  "Read the pseudocode as a state-transition contract. Removing X changes OPEN; goal testing may terminate; generating children invokes the successor function; moving X to CLOSED records examination; duplicate filtering protects the graph invariant; and left insertion creates stack behavior. Each line owns one effect. If an implementation produces the wrong trace, compare these effects one at a time. Debugging six responsibilities at once is like asking which ingredient ruined a soup after adding the pantry with a shovel.",
  "OPEN and CLOSED answer different temporal questions. OPEN says which generated states are still waiting; CLOSED says which states have already been examined. A parent table answers a third question: how did each surviving state first enter the search? Picture an airport with a departure board, a passport stamp, and a baggage claim tag. Mixing those records loses either pending work, duplicate protection, or the route home. One heroic list cannot safely perform every administrative job.",
  "The interactive trace is an instrument panel, not a slot machine. Before each click, predict the selected state, the exact ordered OPEN list, the CLOSED update, and any new parent links. Then one mismatch identifies one faulty rule. If only the next node is predicted, a broken duplicate check can hide for several iterations. Good tracing converts surprise into diagnosis. Bad tracing repeatedly clicks Next and hopes understanding will arrive by express shipping.",
  "BFS is the airport agent who boards passengers by zone: everyone in zone one goes before anyone in zone two, even if a zone-two passenger waves enthusiastically. FIFO order preserves that layer discipline because older shallow states remain ahead of newly generated deeper ones. This yields a shallowest-goal guarantee under finite branching. It does not magically minimize dollars, fuel, or danger; depth counts actions. A cheap six-step route can lose to an expensive five-step route unless costs are equal.",
  "The DFS and BFS procedures differ at a deceptively small hinge: which end receives the children. Imagine a cafeteria line. Place new arrivals at the front and the newest family dominates the next decision, producing stack-like depth. Place them at the rear and older customers retain priority, producing queue-like breadth. The rest of the restaurant can be identical. This is a useful software lesson: a one-line data-structure policy can change global behavior more dramatically than a page of decorative comments.",
  "A BFS OPEN trace should look layered even when node names are distracting. Annotate every state with depth and check that the depths never decrease from left to right. Within one depth, parent expansion and child order break ties. If a depth-two node jumps ahead of an unexamined depth-one node, either children were inserted on the wrong end or the queue was reordered. Alphabetical sorting is especially sneaky: it looks tidy while quietly replacing the algorithm with office filing.",
  "Use the live trace to test conservation of information. One state leaves OPEN, that state enters CLOSED, legal novel children enter OPEN on the right, and each child receives a parent. Nothing should teleport between lists. If two children are generated but only one appears, ask whether the other was a duplicate before calling it missing. A trace is a small accounting ledger; unlike creative accounting, every state should have a documented reason for appearing or disappearing.",
  "The eight-puzzle makes duplicate detection unavoidable because moves are reversible. Slide the blank right and then left, and the board returns wearing no disguise at all. Tree search may treat that return as a new child and reproduce an infinite family reunion. Graph search checks OPEN and CLOSED, retaining the useful first or cheapest record according to policy. Parent pointers then compress the explored mess into one solution path. The search can be large; the submitted directions should not read like its autobiography.",
  "Compare BFS and DFS as two packing styles. BFS rents a warehouse so every state in the current layer remains accessible; DFS carries a backpack containing the current path and nearby alternatives. The warehouse makes shallow discovery dependable but can bankrupt memory. The backpack travels lightly but may wander far from the nearest goal. Neither is morally superior. Asking which is better without branching factor, goal depth, memory budget, and cost structure is like comparing a canoe with a forklift using only color.",
  "Algorithm selection resembles medical triage: begin with the outcome that must be protected. If a shallowest equal-cost solution is required, BFS may justify its memory bill. If memory is strict and any solution is acceptable, DFS may be reasonable. Unknown depth favors iterative deepening; variable positive costs favor UCS. State the accepted risk explicitly. Choosing an algorithm because it was first in the lecture notes is not engineering; it is alphabetical destiny.",
  "A depth bound acts like an elevator whose buttons stop at floor five. Reaching floor five and finding no goal does not prove the building ends; it proves this elevator refuses the next floor. Iterative deepening repeatedly installs one more button. That sounds wasteful, yet broad trees concentrate most nodes near the bottom, so replaying the small lobby levels can cost less than storing an entire crowd. The distinction between cutoff and failure prevents the elevator from making architectural claims.",
  "On the depth-five tree, mark every node with both its depth and its status: expanded, generated but cut off, or never reached. A node at the limit can be goal-tested while its children remain forbidden. This distinction matters in code because a cutoff must propagate differently from failure. If all branches fail within the bound, report failure; if at least one branch is truncated, report cutoff. Otherwise iterative deepening may stop early and confidently announce that floor six has been canceled.",
  "UCS is a road-trip planner that watches toll receipts rather than counting intersections. A path with three roads can cost more than a path with six. The priority queue therefore orders states by accumulated g(n), and a cheaper rediscovery may update an existing entry. Positive lower-bounded costs matter because they prevent infinitely many almost-free detours before the goal. The first goal sign seen through the windshield is not enough; optimality arrives when the goal is the cheapest item removed from the queue.",
  "Read the guarantee matrix as a legal contract with the conditions printed at normal font size. Completeness and optimality are not personality traits that an algorithm carries everywhere. They are implications: if branching, depth, and costs satisfy stated assumptions, then the guarantee follows. Complexity symbols identify the variables driving growth, not a stopwatch prediction. Big O is a map of the storm system; it does not promise that Tuesday at three o'clock will merely be breezy.",
  "Backtracking turns failure into routing information. The current path is a stack of commitments; alternatives are unopened doors attached to earlier commitments; dead ends record doors not worth reopening. When the current state fails, retract only far enough to reach the most recent viable alternative. This is more precise than restarting at the entrance and more disciplined than choosing a random door. The maze may judge our choices silently, but DE ensures it does not get to repeat the same joke.",
  "The four records divide responsibilities. SL represents the active path, CS names its current endpoint, NSL preserves candidate alternatives, and DE remembers recognized dead ends. Test the invariant that CS agrees with the active end of SL after every update. When failure occurs, DE grows while SL contracts; NSL determines the next candidate. If all four lists change arbitrarily, the algorithm is not backtracking—it is a spreadsheet experiencing an emotional event.",
  "Logical recovery is different from physical reversal. A planner can erase a hypothetical suffix from SL in microseconds without commanding a robot to drive backward through the warehouse. Only the final chosen action sequence must be executed. This separation is common in AI: internal search explores possibilities that the external agent never performs. Confusing planning states with physical motion can multiply execution cost and produce a robot that appears thoughtful mainly because it keeps returning to the same shelf.",
  "While scrubbing the trace, treat SL and DE like coupled gauges. A failed suffix should leave the path and enter the dead-end record; the selected alternative should come from the nearest surviving ancestor with options. Pause at iterations three, five, and eight and narrate the exact cause of each change. The slider is useful because it exposes history. Dragging it rapidly back and forth without reading the lists is technically interaction, much as shaking a textbook is technically using gravity.",
  "Graph DFS and explicit backtracking can share a depth-oriented visiting order while maintaining different bookkeeping. DFS leaves alternatives distributed across OPEN and prevents repeats through global history. The backtracking formulation foregrounds one current path, its local alternatives, and dead ends. Similar next states do not imply interchangeable code. Two kitchens can serve the same sandwich while one uses a ticket queue and the other relies on a chef remembering every table—a comparison that becomes exciting during lunch rush.",
  "The five-stage pipeline protects meaning as the solution moves toward code. Real goals and constraints determine the state variables; legal actions determine arcs; data structures preserve frontier, history, cost, and parent information; the algorithm updates those records; the output returns the required goal or path. An error upstream survives every elegant optimization downstream. A priority queue cannot repair a state representation that forgot fuel, just as a faster oven cannot restore an ingredient omitted from the recipe.",
  "The review tabs ask for layered competence. Explanation states what a concept means; tracing predicts concrete state changes; implementation maps those changes to data structures; critique identifies assumptions and failure modes. Practice one weak layer directly. If queue behavior is unclear, write OPEN after each expansion. If guarantees are unclear, add the missing conditions. Re-reading the acronym ten times may create familiarity, but familiarity is the academic cousin of recognizing someone at a party and still not knowing their name.",
  "Use the outcomes as preflight checks. Can you model the problem, define operators, choose data structures, trace DFS and BFS, implement parent recovery, and diagnose backtracking failure? A yes should be supported by an example or trace, not optimistic weather. The outcomes connect representation to process because search errors often masquerade: a bad state model looks like an algorithm bug, and a missing parent table looks like a successful search that forgot why it came.",
  "The exit task is a mechanic's diagnosis, not an algorithm popularity poll. A loop suggests duplicate handling; unbounded descent suggests a limit or deepening; memory explosion suggests breadth pressure; a cheap deeper path suggests cost ordering. Name the failure, identify the violated condition, and choose the smallest repair that addresses it. Installing UCS because the search engine made a strange noise is like replacing a car's transmission because the cup holder squeaks—expensive, impressive, and unrelated.",
];
